This `useEffect` starts an async operation (`fetchCorpayFields(...).finally(...)`) that calls `setCompletedResumeFieldsKey(resumeFieldsKey)` when it resolves, but there is no cleanup to discard a stale response. `resumeFieldsKey` is captured at effect run time, so if the effect re-runs with a different key (e.g. the user changes country/currency) before the first request settles, the earlier `.finally()` can overwrite `completedResumeFieldsKey` with a stale value. The `requestedResumeFieldsKeyRef` guard only prevents duplicate requests for the same key; it does not cancel an in-flight one.

Add an `ignore` flag (or `AbortController`) and check it before the `setState`:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (isLoading || !shouldRefreshResumeFields || requestedResumeFieldsKeyRef.current === resumeFieldsKey || !draftValues?.bankCountry) {
        return;
    }
    let ignore = false;
    requestedResumeFieldsKeyRef.current = resumeFieldsKey;
    fetchCorpayFields(draftValues.bankCountry, draftValues.bankCurrency, false, false, {preserveExistingDraft: true}).finally(() => {
        if (!ignore) {
            setCompletedResumeFieldsKey(resumeFieldsKey);
        }
    });
    return () => {
        ignore = true;
    };
}, [draftValues?.bankCountry, draftValues?.bankCurrency, isLoading, resumeFieldsKey, shouldRefreshResumeFields]);
```

This effect calls `calculatePopoverPosition(...).then(setMoreMenuAnchorPosition)` with no cleanup. Because `calculatePopoverPosition` is async (resolves from `measureInWindow`), a stale in-flight measurement can overwrite a newer one when the dependencies change, mispositioning the "More" menu.

Add an `ignore` flag checked before `setMoreMenuAnchorPosition`, and clear it in the effect's cleanup:
```tsx
let ignore = false;
calculatePopoverPosition(moreAnchorRef, MORE_MENU_ANCHOR_ALIGNMENT).then((position) => {
    if (!ignore) {
        setMoreMenuAnchorPosition(position);
    }
});
return () => {
    ignore = true;
};
```

This effect awaits an async result (`calculatePopoverPosition` returns a Promise resolved from `measureInWindow`) and calls `setAnchorPosition` in `.then()` with no cleanup. If `isMenuVisible`/`calculatePopoverPosition` change while a measurement is in flight, a slower earlier response can overwrite a newer one, mispositioning the popover.

Guard the state update with an `ignore` flag:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (!anchorRef.current || !isMenuVisible) {
        return;
    }
    let ignore = false;
    calculatePopoverPosition(anchorRef, SUB_MENU_ANCHOR_ALIGNMENT).then((position) => {
        if (!ignore) {
            setAnchorPosition(position);
        }
    });
    return () => {
        ignore = true;
    };
}, [isMenuVisible, calculatePopoverPosition]);
```

This `useFocusEffect` kicks off an async request (`getTravelRiskApproval(policyID)`) and, when it resolves, performs a side effect (`clearTravelSignupRequest()`, an Onyx mutation) with no cleanup to discard a stale response. The effect depends on `policyID`, so if it changes (or the screen loses focus) before the promise resolves, an in-flight response tied to a previous `policyID` can still fire `clearTravelSignupRequest()`, mutating state based on outdated data.

Add an `ignore` flag that the cleanup function flips, and check it before mutating:

```tsx
useFocusEffect(
    useCallback(() => {
        let ignore = false;
        getTravelRiskApproval(policyID).then((isTravelRiskApproved) => {
            if (ignore || !isTravelRiskApproved) {
                return;
            }
            clearTravelSignupRequest();
        });
        return () => {
            ignore = true;
        };
    }, [policyID]),
);
```

This `useEffect` dispatches `openReport` (an API request plus optimistic Onyx mutations) and its dependency array is annotated with `// eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps`. This PR adds the newly captured `currentUserAccountID` into the effect body, but the suppressed lint keeps it out of the dependency array `[shouldUseNarrowLayout, props.route.params.reportID]`. A later change to `currentUserAccountID` (e.g. delegate/account switch) will not re-run the effect, so `openReport` may run with a stale account ID and build optimistic data for the wrong user.

Either include `currentUserAccountID` in the dependency array, or keep it suppressed with an explicit comment explaining why the account ID is safe to omit here:

```tsx
openReport({
    reportID: props.route.params.reportID,
    introSelected,
    betas,
    currentUserAccountID,
});
}, [shouldUseNarrowLayout, props.route.params.reportID, currentUserAccountID]);
```

This `useEffect` dispatches `openReport` (which triggers an API request and optimistic Onyx mutations) and its dependency array is annotated with `// eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps`. This PR adds a new captured value, `currentUserAccountID`, to the effect body, but the suppressed lint hides it from the dependency array `[shouldFetchReport, isReportLoaded, reportID]`. If `currentUserAccountID` changes (e.g. delegate/account switch) while this HOC stays mounted, the effect will keep firing `openReport` with a stale account ID, producing optimistic data attributed to the wrong user.

Either add `currentUserAccountID` to the dependency array, or narrow the suppression and document why the account ID is intentionally excluded:

```tsx
openReport({reportID, introSelected, betas, currentUserAccountID});
}, [shouldFetchReport, isReportLoaded, reportID, currentUserAccountID]);
```

This change removes the `ignore` flag and the cleanup function from a `useFocusEffect` that performs async work (`getForegroundPermissionsAsync().then(...)` and `getCurrentPosition(...)`). Both async callbacks perform side effects via `setState` (`setCurrentPositionToInitialState` and `setUserLocation`). Without the cleanup mechanism, if the effect re-runs (dependencies change, or the screen re-focuses) or the component unmounts while a permission/location request is still in flight, a stale earlier response can overwrite fresher state — the exact race condition PERF-15 guards against. This is why the `ignore` flag existed.

Keep the `ignore` flag and cleanup so stale in-flight responses are discarded:

```tsx
let ignore = false;
getForegroundPermissionsAsync().then(({granted}) => {
    if (ignore) {
        return;
    }
    // ...
    getCurrentPosition((params) => {
        if (ignore) {
            return;
        }
        setUserLocation(currentCoords);
    }, setCurrentPositionToInitialState);
});

return () => {
    ignore = true;
};
```

If removal is intentional, please justify why the race condition can no longer occur (e.g., `shouldInitializeCurrentPosition.current` guard is sufficient).

`fetchAuthenticatedUri` is invoked from a `useEffect` (and from `useNetwork`'s `onReconnect`) and calls `getShortLivedAuthTokenURL(uri).then(setAuthenticatedUri)` with no mechanism to discard the response if the component unmounts before the token resolves. The token request is a network round-trip, so the user can navigate away (back button) while it's in flight — when it resolves, `setAuthenticatedUri` runs on an unmounted component. The `hasFetchedAuthenticatedUri` ref only prevents a second fetch; it does not guard the `setState` against a late/stale response.

Add an `ignore` flag (or `AbortController`) that is checked before calling `setAuthenticatedUri`, and clear it in the effect's cleanup:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (isOffline) {
        return;
    }
    let ignore = false;
    if (shouldAppendShortLivedAuthToken && !hasFetchedAuthenticatedUri.current) {
        hasFetchedAuthenticatedUri.current = true;
        getShortLivedAuthTokenURL(uri).then((url) => {
            if (!ignore) {
                setAuthenticatedUri(url);
            }
        });
    }
    return () => {
        ignore = true;
    };
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps -- only fetch once on mount when online
}, []);
```

Apply the same guarding to the `onReconnect` path so a reconnect-triggered fetch also cannot call `setState` after unmount.

This newly added `getForegroundPermissionsAsync().then(...)` runs inside a `useFocusEffect` and performs state-mutating side effects in the async callback (`setCurrentPositionToInitialState()` and, via `getCurrentPosition`, `setUserLocation(...)`), but has no cleanup to discard a stale/in-flight response. If the screen loses focus or unmounts before the promise resolves, the callback can still fire `setState`, and the sibling `GPSMapView.tsx` already guards the identical pattern with an `ignore` flag.

Add an `ignore` flag and return a cleanup function from the focus-effect callback, matching `GPSMapView.tsx`:

```tsx
useFocusEffect(
    useCallback(() => {
        // ...existing early returns...
        let ignore = false;
        getForegroundPermissionsAsync().then(({granted}) => {
            if (ignore) {
                return;
            }
            if (!granted) {
                setCurrentPositionToInitialState();
                return;
            }
            getCurrentPosition((params) => {
                if (ignore) {
                    return;
                }
                setUserLocation({longitude: params.coords.longitude, latitude: params.coords.latitude});
            }, setCurrentPositionToInitialState);
        });
        return () => {
            ignore = true;
        };
    }, [isOffline, shouldPanMapToCurrentPosition, setCurrentPositionToInitialState]),
);
```

The deferred work inside this `useEffect` performs a navigation side effect (`Navigation.navigate`) via a `setTimeout`, but there is no cancellation mechanism (no `clearTimeout`, `ignore` flag, or `AbortController`). Because the effect depends on rapidly-changing values (`achData?.state`, `policyCurrency`, `isPreviousPolicy`, etc.), a stale deferred navigation can still fire after the dependencies change or the page unmounts, pushing the validation route at the wrong time. The comment acknowledges the unmount race, which confirms the risk.

Return a cleanup that cancels the pending navigation so a stale scheduled push cannot execute:

```tsx
const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
    Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.BANK_ACCOUNT_USD_SETUP.getRoute({policyID: policyIDParam, page: CONST.BANK_ACCOUNT.PAGE_NAMES.VALIDATION, backTo}));
}, 0);
return () => clearTimeout(timeoutId);
```

This effect performs side effects (`readNewestAction`, which triggers an Onyx optimistic merge + API write and a `DeviceEventEmitter.emit`) while its dependency array suppresses `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps`. It reads many values that are absent from the deps (`report`, `transactionThreadReport`, `sortedVisibleReportActions`, `lastAction`, `currentUserAccountID`, `isReportArchived`, `isScrolledToEnd`). Suppressing the lint here hides dependencies that can change and cause the report to be marked read against stale action/read-time state — the race condition this rule guards against. The PR reverts away from `useEffectEvent`, which is React's recommended way to read the latest values inside an effect without widening the dependency array.

Prefer keeping the `useEffectEvent` form so the latest report/action values are read safely without suppressing the lint:

```tsx
const handleAppVisibilityMarkAsRead = useEffectEvent(() => {
    // ...reads latest report/actions safely...
});

useEffect(() => {
    handleAppVisibilityMarkAsRead();
}, [isVisible, isFocused]);
```

If the plain-`useEffect` form must remain, include the omitted dependencies (or capture them via refs) instead of disabling `exhaustive-deps`.

This effect performs side effects (a data mutation via `readNewestAction`, an Onyx optimistic update + API write, and navigation via `Navigation.setParams`), but its dependency array suppresses `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps`. The suppression hides real dependencies that are read inside the effect but omitted from the array (`report`, `transactionThreadReport`, `sortedVisibleReportActions`, `route`, `hasNewerActions`, `isScrolledToEnd`, `currentUserAccountID`, `isReportArchived`). Because the effect reads these values through a stale closure, an update to any of them can lead to the report being marked read against outdated state — the exact race condition this rule targets. This PR reverts away from `useEffectEvent`, which is React's recommended primitive for reading the latest values inside an effect without adding them to the dependency array.

Prefer keeping the `useEffectEvent` version (the previous code), which reads the latest report/action values safely without suppressing the lint:

```tsx
const handleReportChangeMarkAsRead = useEffectEvent(() => {
    // ...reads latest report/lastAction/route/etc. safely...
});

useEffect(() => {
    handleReportChangeMarkAsRead();
}, [report?.lastVisibleActionCreated, transactionThreadReport?.lastVisibleActionCreated, reportID, isVisible, isReportActionsLoaded]);
```

If the plain-`useEffect` form must stay, add the missing dependencies (or refs) rather than suppressing `exhaustive-deps`, so a change in `report`/`route`/`isScrolledToEnd` cannot mark a report read against stale state.

This `useEffect` performs async work (`getContactPermission().then(async ...)` and an awaited `showConfirmModal`) that triggers side effects — `onGrant`/`onDeny`, `setHasDeniedContactImportPrompt`, and `onFocusTextInput`. There is no cleanup mechanism to discard a stale resolution if the hook's host component unmounts (or its inputs change) while the permission/modal promise is still in flight, and the `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` lint is suppressed, hiding dependencies that could change between invocations.

Guard the async continuation with an `ignore` flag cleared on unmount so stale callbacks don't fire after teardown:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;
    if (hasDeniedContactImportPrompt) {
        onFocusTextInput();
        return;
    }
    getContactPermission().then(async (status) => {
        if (ignore) {
            return;
        }
        // ...
    });
    return () => {
        ignore = true;
    };
}, []);
```

This mount-time `useEffect` kicks off an async modal flow (`showConfirmModal(...).then(...)`) whose resolution performs destructive side effects — `resetUSDBankAccount`/`resetNonUSDBankAccount`, parent state setters, and `Navigation.navigate` — but the effect has no cleanup/cancellation mechanism. If this component unmounts while the confirmation modal is still open, the resolved `.then` callback can still run `handleConfirm()`/`cancelResetBankAccount()` against a torn-down component, firing mutations and navigation after unmount.

Add an `ignore`/cancellation guard so stale resolutions are discarded:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;
    showResetModal(() => ignore); // or check `ignore` inside the .then before mutating/navigating
    return () => {
        ignore = true;
    };
}, []);
```

or have `showResetModal` capture a cancellation flag and check it inside the `.then(({action}) => { ... })` callback before calling `handleConfirm()`/`cancelResetBankAccount()`.

This effect performs async work (`setAccessToken(accessToken).then(...)`) and calls `setIsAccessTokenSet(true)` in the callback, but has no cleanup to discard a stale response. Since `accessToken` is a dependency, if it changes while an earlier `setAccessToken` is still in flight, the slower earlier resolution can run `setState` after the effect has been re-run/unmounted.

Add an `ignore` flag cleared on cleanup:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;
    setAccessToken(accessToken).then((token) => {
        if (ignore || !token) {
            return;
        }
        setIsAccessTokenSet(true);
    });
    return () => {
        ignore = true;
    };
}, [accessToken]);
```

This `useFocusEffect` kicks off an async `requestForegroundPermissionsAsync()` and calls `setForegroundLocationPermissionsGranted` in the `.then` callback, but there is no cleanup to discard a stale resolution. Because `useFocusEffect` re-runs on every refocus, an in-flight permission request can resolve after the screen has unfocused/unmounted (or after a newer request), causing a setState on a stale effect run.

Guard the state update with an `ignore` flag cleared on cleanup:

```tsx
useFocusEffect(
    useCallback(() => {
        let ignore = false;
        requestForegroundPermissionsAsync().then(({granted}) => {
            if (ignore) {
                return;
            }
            setForegroundLocationPermissionsGranted(granted);
        });
        return () => {
            ignore = true;
        };
    }, []),
);
```

`Navigation.isNavigationReady().then(() => navigationRef.reset(...))` is an async operation inside a `useEffect` with dependencies `[canCompleteTwoFactorOnSignIn, exitTo]`, but there is no cleanup to discard the stale callback. If `canCompleteTwoFactorOnSignIn` flips to `false` (or `exitTo` changes) before `isNavigationReady()` resolves, the `.then()` callback will still fire and reset the navigation stack unexpectedly.

Add an `ignore` flag checked inside the `.then()` callback, and return a cleanup function that sets it:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (canCompleteTwoFactorOnSignIn) {
        let ignore = false;
        Navigation.isNavigationReady().then(() => {
            if (!ignore) {
                navigationRef.reset({index: 0, routes: [{name: SCREENS.HOME}]});
            }
        });
        return () => { ignore = true; };
    }

    if (exitTo) {
        handleExitToNavigation(exitTo);
    }
}, [canCompleteTwoFactorOnSignIn, exitTo]);
```

This `useEffect` calls `Navigation.isNavigationReady().then(...)` which performs side effects (`setHasInitiatedSAMLLogin(true)`, mutation of `hasPendingSAMLRedirectForCurrentSignIn`, and `Navigation.navigate(...)`) inside the `.then()` callback. If `shouldInitiateSAMLLogin` toggles rapidly or the component unmounts before the promise resolves, the stale callback will still execute, potentially navigating to the SAML sign-in page after the effect is no longer relevant.

Add a cleanup mechanism using an `ignore` flag to prevent stale promise resolution:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (!shouldInitiateSAMLLogin) {
        return;
    }

    let ignore = false;

    Navigation.isNavigationReady().then(() => {
        if (ignore) {
            return;
        }
        setHasInitiatedSAMLLogin(true);
        hasPendingSAMLRedirectForCurrentSignIn = false;
        Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.SAML_SIGN_IN);
    });

    return () => {
        ignore = true;
    };
}, [shouldInitiateSAMLLogin]);
```

`TransitionTracker.runAfterTransitions` with `waitForUpcomingTransition: true` is asynchronous (it awaits a Promise internally), but the returned `CancelHandle` is not used to clean up on dependency changes. When `isFocused` or `deleteTransactionNavigateBackUrl` changes, the previous pending callback is not cancelled, so a stale callback from the prior render can still fire.

Store the returned handle and cancel it in the effect cleanup:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    // ... guards ...
    const handle = TransitionTracker.runAfterTransitions({
        callback: () => {
            requestAnimationFrame(clearDeleteTransactionNavigateBackUrl);
        },
        waitForUpcomingTransition: true,
    });
    return () => handle.cancel();
}, [isFocused, deleteTransactionNavigateBackUrl]);
```

