`showConfirmModal` returns a Promise, and the `.then()` callback performs navigation side effects (`Navigation.closeRHPFlow()` and `Navigation.navigate()`). This runs inside a `useEffect` with many dependencies. If those dependencies change while the modal is still open (e.g., `isNewFeedConnected` or `isDuplicateFeed` toggles), the effect re-fires but the previous Promise's `.then()` callback will still resolve and execute navigation with stale closure values, causing a race condition.

Add an `ignore` flag with cleanup to discard stale resolutions:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;
    // ...
    if (isDuplicateFeed) {
        showConfirmModal({ ... }).then(() => {
            if (ignore) return;
            Navigation.closeRHPFlow();
            Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.WORKSPACE_COMPANY_CARDS.getRoute(policyID), {forceReplace: true});
        });
        return;
    }
    // ...
    return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [/* deps */]);
```

`Navigation.isNavigationReady().then(() => Navigation.navigate(...))` is an async operation performing navigation (a non-idempotent side effect) inside a `useEffect` without any cleanup mechanism. If the dependencies (`isUserValidated`, `is2FAEnabled`, `isFocused`) change while `isNavigationReady()` is still pending, the stale `.then()` callback will still fire and trigger an unwanted navigation.

Add an `ignore` flag to discard stale callbacks:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;

    if (!isUserValidated) {
        Navigation.isNavigationReady().then(() => {
            if (!ignore) {
                Navigation.navigate(createDynamicRoute(...), {forceReplace: true});
            }
        });
        return () => { ignore = true; };
    }

    if (isFocused && is2FAEnabled) {
        Navigation.isNavigationReady().then(() => {
            if (!ignore) {
                Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.SETTINGS_2FA_ENABLED, {forceReplace: true});
            }
        });
        return () => { ignore = true; };
    }
    // ... rest of effect
    return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [isUserValidated, accountMetadata.status, isFocused, is2FAEnabled]);
```

This `useEffect` calls `Navigation.isNavigationReady().then(...)` which performs `setState` (`setActiveRouteWithoutParams`) upon resolution, but has no cleanup mechanism. If the component unmounts before the promise resolves, `setState` will be called on an unmounted component. This is a race condition risk.

Add an `ignore` flag to discard stale results:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;
    Navigation.isNavigationReady().then(() => {
        if (!ignore) {
            setActiveRouteWithoutParams(Navigation.getActiveRouteWithoutParams());
        }
    });
    return () => {
        ignore = true;
    };
}, []);
```

The `waitForDeferredFlush(...).then(fireSearch)` call inside this `useEffect` is an async operation with no cleanup mechanism. If the effect's dependencies change (e.g., `hash`, `queryJSON`) while the promise is still pending, the old `.then(fireSearch)` callback will still execute with stale closure values, potentially firing a duplicate or outdated search request.

Add an `ignore` flag to discard stale resolutions:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;
    // ... existing guards ...
    const fireSearch = () => search({queryJSON, searchKey: currentSearchKey, offset: 0, shouldCalculateTotals, isLoading: false, skipWaitForWrites: false});

    if (hasDeferredWrite(CONST.DEFERRED_LAYOUT_WRITE_KEYS.SEARCH)) {
        waitForDeferredFlush(CONST.DEFERRED_LAYOUT_WRITE_KEYS.SEARCH).then(() => {
            if (!ignore) {
                fireSearch();
            }
        });
        return () => { ignore = true; };
    }
    fireSearch();
}, [hash, isOffline, shouldUseLiveData, queryJSON, isSnapshotDataLoaded, isSnapshotSearchLoading, currentSearchKey, shouldCalculateTotals]);
```

This `useEffect` calls `getLocationPermission().then(...)` which triggers `getCurrentPosition` and `setUserLocation`. The dependency array `[transaction?.amount, iouType]` means this effect can re-fire. If the deps change while the previous async chain is still in flight, the stale callback will still execute `clearUserLocation()` and `setUserLocation()`, potentially overwriting a more recent result.

Add an ignore flag to discard stale responses:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;
    const gpsRequired = transaction?.amount === 0 && iouType !== CONST.IOU.TYPE.SPLIT;
    if (!gpsRequired) {
        return;
    }

    getLocationPermission().then((status) => {
        if (ignore || (status !== RESULTS.GRANTED && status !== RESULTS.LIMITED)) {
            return;
        }
        clearUserLocation();
        getCurrentPosition(
            (successData) => {
                if (!ignore) {
                    setUserLocation({longitude: successData.coords.longitude, latitude: successData.coords.latitude});
                }
            },
            () => {},
        );
    });

    return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [transaction?.amount, iouType]);
```

The `refreshCameraPermissionStatus` function called in this `useEffect` performs async work (`.then(setCameraPermissionStatus)`) but there is no cleanup to prevent stale state updates. If `isVisible` toggles rapidly (e.g., user opens and closes the camera modal quickly), the promise from the previous invocation could resolve after the modal is hidden and set stale permission status.

Add an `ignore` flag to discard stale responses:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (!isVisible) {
        return;
    }
    let ignore = false;
    CameraPermission?.getCameraPermissionStatus?.()
        .then((status) => {
            if (!ignore) {
                setCameraPermissionStatus(status);
            }
        })
        .catch(() => {
            if (!ignore) {
                setCameraPermissionStatus(RESULTS.UNAVAILABLE);
            }
        });
    return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [isVisible]);
```

This `useEffect` performs async work via `queryCameraPermission().then(...)` which calls `setCameraPermissionState` and `setIsQueriedPermissionState`, but has no cleanup mechanism to discard stale responses. When `isTabActive` toggles rapidly (e.g., quick tab switches), a slow earlier promise can resolve after a faster later one and overwrite the correct permission state.

Add an `ignore` flag to prevent stale responses from updating state:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (!isTabActive) {
        return;
    }
    let ignore = false;
    queryCameraPermission()
        .then((state) => {
            if (ignore) return;
            setCameraPermissionState(state);
            if (state === 'granted') {
                requestCameraPermission();
            }
        })
        .catch(() => {
            if (ignore) return;
            setCameraPermissionState('denied');
        })
        .finally(() => {
            if (ignore) return;
            setIsQueriedPermissionState(true);
        });
    return () => { ignore = true; };
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
}, [isTabActive]);
```

`Navigation.waitForProtectedRoutes().then(() => startOnboardingFlow(...))` is an async operation inside a `useEffect` with no cleanup mechanism. If the effect's dependencies change (there are many in the dependency array) before the Promise resolves, `startOnboardingFlow` will still fire with stale closure values, potentially triggering a navigation to onboarding when it should no longer apply. Since `startOnboardingFlow` performs navigation (a non-idempotent mutation), this is a race condition.

Add an `ignore` flag that is set to `true` in the cleanup function, and check it before calling `startOnboardingFlow`:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;
    // ... existing logic ...
    if (isOnboardingCompleted === false && ...) {
        Navigation.waitForProtectedRoutes().then(() => {
            if (ignore) return;
            startOnboardingFlow({...});
        });
    }
    return () => {
        ignore = true;
        handle.cancel();
    };
}, [/* deps */]);
```

This `useEffect` performs async work via `Promise.all(...).then(...)` that triggers side effects -- `onReceiptFilesChange` (setState), `Navigation.navigate(...)`, `removeDraftTransactionsByIDs(...)`, and `navigateToStartMoneyRequestStep(...)` -- but has no cleanup mechanism. If any dependency changes while the Promise chain is still in flight, the stale `.then()` callback will still execute, potentially navigating to the wrong route or setting state on an outdated context.

Add an `ignore` flag checked before every side effect in the `.then()` block:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;
    let newReceiptFiles: Record<string, Receipt> = {};
    let isScanFilesCanBeRead = true;

    Promise.all(
        transactions.map((item) => {
            // ... existing mapping logic ...
        }),
    ).then(() => {
        if (ignore) {
            return;
        }
        if (isScanFilesCanBeRead) {
            onReceiptFilesChange(newReceiptFiles);
            return;
        }
        // ... rest of the then block
    });

    return () => {
        ignore = true;
    };
}, [/* deps */]);
```

